#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130071} :activity/id #uuid "40540042-d3fd-5add-a52b-682e9eaa6e90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048986 :topic/id #uuid "d11c4451-1bcb-5f1a-a6d3-3f5fdbe85bb7" :topic/math-academy-id 1997} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130399 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\color{black}0}^{\\color{black}2} \\int_{\\color{black}x/2}^{\\color{black}1} \\, e^{y^2}\\,\\textrm{d}{\\color{black}y} \\, \\textrm{d}{\\color{black}x}.\n$$\n\nThe expression \"$\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x\\,$\" tells us that we should integrate first with respect to $y.$ This is problematic, though, as the integrand $e^{y^2}$ does not have an elementary antiderivative. So, what can we do?\n\nIn cases like this, **reversing the order of integration** from $\\, \\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x\\,$ to $\\, \\textrm{d}x\\,\\textrm{d}y$ might help. But beware! Because the domain of integration is non-rectangular, we can't just swap the order of the integrals. This is because, to integrate with respect to $x,$ we can't have $x$'s in the limits. So we need to work out new limits.\n\nWhen swapping the order of integration, it is helpful to sketch the region $D$ over which we're integrating.\n\n![](images/d3/d3ee76083ae3917b4585fce7227475d3e0471550f193f2fa437be3ebf92dc288.png)\n\nTo integrate first with respect to $y,$ we imagine a line parallel to the $y$-axis, as shown above. Note the following:\n\n- This line *enters* the region through the curve $\\color{black} y=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ and *leaves* at $\\color{black} y=1.$ These are the variable limits for $y.$\n\n- The minimum value of $x$ is $\\color{black}0,$ and the maximum value of $x$ is ${\\color{black}2}.$\n\nSo, the integral we were initially given is over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\left \\{  (x,y) \\: : \\: {\\color{black}0} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{black}2}, \\quad {\\color{black}\\dfrac{x}{2}} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{black}1} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we now want to integrate first with respect to $x,$ we need the limits for $x$ to be functions of $y$, and we need the limits of $y$ to be constants. To work these out, we redraw the region, but this time we imagine a line parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/c8/c80ae995a92aba4f535b5e5a01305f8ffbf790ad1f97ee8db4f368e4bfabb0c1.png)\n\n- The line *enters* the region at $\\color{black}x=0$ and *leaves* the region at $\\color{black} x=2y.$ So these are our variable limits for $x.$\n\n- The minimum value of $\\color{black}y$ is $\\color{black}0,$ and the maximum value of $y$ is ${\\color{black}1}.$\n\nSo, we can consider $D$ to be the following type II region:\n\n$$\nD=\\left \\{  (x,y):\\quad {\\color{black}0} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{black}2y}, \\quad {\\color{black}0} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{black}1} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressed the region $D$ as a type II region, we're ready to swap the order of integration and finally calculate our integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{1}\\,{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{y}^{2}}[x]_{0}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y{e}^{{y}^{2}}dy \\\\  & ={[{e}^{{y}^{2}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ccfd99d2-4e17-53c3-8ac5-2394c59be5c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4357 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e13e6db5-65c2-53ea-9644-faae6ec26f16" :step/math-academy-id 26536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129189 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129008 :question/id #uuid "9b2415da-21b8-5a59-9b3f-2f30b624c36f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7194"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1723a1-c676-517c-8dc5-f2b61ba017c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488189}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "d45a7233-e073-55c6-8a71-375178f2f11a" :step/math-academy-id 26537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129190 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129009 :question/id #uuid "3477b5ca-309c-5b2a-b3fd-41dfc9bbf4db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7195"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049003}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544876}] :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"} :step/id #uuid "981fea3c-a71a-5567-bcaf-63176b8545f5" :step/math-academy-id 26538 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130072}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129010 :question/id #uuid "eab89095-f5e5-53dc-9141-0f1a876f8a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7196"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475945}] :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"} :step/id #uuid "768398f5-6108-549e-9288-fd51dc694028" :step/math-academy-id 26540 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130073}}] :activity/title "Changing the Order of Integration in Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129342}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488297} :activity/id #uuid "bf5c1cdc-1231-5945-ae88-c7df3fc76214" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048986 :topic/id #uuid "d11c4451-1bcb-5f1a-a6d3-3f5fdbe85bb7" :topic/math-academy-id 1997} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab8fcd9-4602-5070-8690-3160b7afea7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487621} {:answer/id #uuid "295507dd-328a-5bb3-964f-3c0f304a9d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487650} {:answer/id #uuid "335927a5-38d1-5363-b0b7-58dbc7940dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(1-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487664} {:answer/id #uuid "6465b0aa-6d02-553e-acdc-7035e0eb70ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487768} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f6c3ee-d01c-5d14-87c0-fce9876c9b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487650} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eba9098-a15f-5710-a728-b6dfa4ffcdb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "18010b03-c75c-5a16-8ddc-495cf577e016" :question/math-academy-id "q-38799" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/54/54727a51e88b3543726b61606a0f41b9a8cf5ec46ef4c69d7233fd54a4b50242.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/95/956aaf81973d9a2b3e56082ecae8e654eee55d6f99c42dd433d126632d7c7262.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{y}^{4}}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{{y}^{4}}}{3}[{x}^{3}]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[{e}^{{y}^{4}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1e312c9f-cb74-5f7f-9c13-ab33fcd2a8b6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13af14b5-15a1-566b-a656-d8a378d7320c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324147} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1a2f03-596f-5e6f-978b-605dcec2e3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324215} {:answer/id #uuid "860bc5a9-e2eb-5f7c-a636-43aba8e0881d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{4-y}^{y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325106} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fec665-57db-5f83-bb6e-f9267a2d10aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{4-y}^{y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325336} {:answer/id #uuid "f2804b9b-bae3-55b0-8ce1-7ac7c4d428a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326107} :answer-field/id #uuid "759c1086-e737-5e21-a10a-cebde7f9cab4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "51957ec1-4df6-5139-b864-fabbb3776e09" :question/math-academy-id "q-38601" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea36f1ec1ddeb03dd8d133fb8d65b5d50b3fa07ba34495978d87f95742dee042.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-x\\,⟹\\,x=4-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e82098a4bb63f1d709a06b6a24da078594716d6a3cc0fa2e069af9dcd9154955.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "32fd6dca-607a-5214-a4a3-fbde7a7bf2c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1215436d-cf83-5f92-b39e-a9bec379f2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y/2}}{\\int }_{(4-y)/2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487602} {:answer/id #uuid "1291e1c1-cc7f-5dab-8331-0c5c3605b71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{(4-y)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487605} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce71b5a-ae72-56f3-907b-b226cfb92e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{(4-y)/2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487782} {:answer/id #uuid "a34f841b-c478-5350-b05a-4956614fb33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{(4-y)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487872} {:answer/id #uuid "a838ae42-337e-55e8-9ec4-76976d1219db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{(4-y)/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc9b43e-629a-5bba-b51a-3bf554da7329" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "291bba76-b18a-519c-8c81-930cc19f299e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82253" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/83/8359a189981b238d0851324a896ed0a3a9aa0620e8cf4d38228752ff484b4cd0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2{x}^{2}\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2(x-2)^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{4-y}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\\le x\\le \\frac{4-y}{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/be/bee5de8ca9011672a159686d1547a909e5288097b15241f5004e5c788459ee70.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2(x-2)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{(4-y)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48d26477-5711-5c8c-9f6f-58692fb49ba5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12862e2a-8176-5480-9c3a-b73349d4eada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487603} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfe33b9-51d1-5788-8662-58bc8ab68510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487684} {:answer/id #uuid "469a96e1-ead2-518e-bb41-99b1ce5ab6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487704} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfda9e5-2b1f-5351-b880-190a9511ab54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487811} {:answer/id #uuid "ff35cb83-3ce5-547e-a2d3-554a83f4034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487704} :answer-field/id #uuid "acad3c64-a048-5588-ba2a-180649362504" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1781d10d-4447-567d-8dfd-9f16c098a159" :question/math-academy-id "q-132647" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d2/d2ef7f656cf706636c82e1ef41ae338a596fff5142962a74875f762301937932.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le y\\le -1,\\,1\\le x\\le -\\frac{4}{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=-1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{y}\\,⟹\\,y=-\\frac{4}{x},\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{4}{x}\\le y\\le -1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/33/3338ee0f8bdc6f18bb5a56b12a36d31d79c87db6c39686adc7ae7c00efb9d526.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-4/x}^{-1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}[{\\int }_{-4/x}^{-1}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{-4/x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{4}{x}-1)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-4+2}-2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-1+2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2}-2 \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "66d3bee5-2c83-50d6-baeb-1f27e2bd6de0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "092a29e0-d74d-5d43-953c-1f5d9bf2a5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388295} {:answer/id #uuid "60cc9f6b-b572-50b9-952f-12e386a01c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388296} {:answer/id #uuid "aea1d3dc-a5bf-519d-8f6d-ded54403dbfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388297} {:answer/id #uuid "133a0e32-0ea5-5f11-8c08-78665b249cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388298} {:answer/id #uuid "81d539a4-fe56-54af-a346-d28ee3e104cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388295} :answer-field/id #uuid "66942a40-bd6e-5606-91e2-d8c0f63814d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9a7843d-3020-5482-8aef-e872811d9165" :question/math-academy-id "q-132565" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/34/3426e7bdd134bc9af5a3665f8c9c55a65514afa9dca5be112eabb37479c4c9ec.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type II regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1-e\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)\\le x\\le 1\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le e-1,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\le x\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,⟹\\,y={e}^{x}-1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)\\,⟹\\,y=1-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1-{e}^{x}\\le y\\le {e}^{x}-1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7d/7d88d351b481859ec3e77350d3679387836d2e7f67325caca50e2f61cce9dcce.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a31ece2d-f9c7-54ca-957f-b6feaf1a1054" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488310}}] :activity/title "Changing the Order of Integration in Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487562}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544913} :activity/id #uuid "4fe3e7fe-66b2-56e4-b07b-e2a8d0dd2aac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048986 :topic/id #uuid "d11c4451-1bcb-5f1a-a6d3-3f5fdbe85bb7" :topic/math-academy-id 1997} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83af4c85-31c8-5333-8b0c-d044a02bd3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{x+1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267303} {:answer/id #uuid "8835a210-9de5-5c5f-a9a0-4264ee64fa8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{x+1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267345} {:answer/id #uuid "bda7f1f6-caae-5b5a-912d-ad3b965431f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{x+1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267847} {:answer/id #uuid "c79e2d71-f72b-5349-9f02-4326a7a438da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{-x-1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267934} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9ca14f-93c5-5b93-88bb-be41c8973bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{-x-1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267934} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe90643-e048-5d75-985f-e9ed17b022bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "88491dc6-a6a9-5e2a-ab57-c63ec8866fd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132574" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}-1}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/92/926e4c717707d13fdb9562e149d2f41c3ab657b42fbae35c0953019ab93a06b6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,-{y}^{2}-1\\le x\\le -y\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](images/89/8933233e8aca4a36ee90ffec69ca6e93d0523d3fa50c400d2cdea78f9ec80812.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $y=y(x),$  are \n\n$\\,$ $y=\\sqrt{-x-1}\\,$ and $\\,y=1.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=-x.$ \n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le -1,\\,\\sqrt{-x-1}\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le -x\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}-1}^{-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{-x-1}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "19c28e52-fdaf-5ad1-b61f-5be844edc12c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1158bf19-5d87-5d3c-ae18-4b6a90a7b40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1+{e}^{-4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544121} {:answer/id #uuid "3290e5fa-709a-5cf8-9205-a80c7e9bb0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1-{e}^{-4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544199} {:answer/id #uuid "8d52f326-a07f-5846-9198-2281c9cb0ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544391} {:answer/id #uuid "fc472a25-8431-51bb-a98c-291448f6d760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544638} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3e3379-5ac5-5f47-9142-c0dd3eb25756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1+e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544199} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ae90e2c-9b42-50f3-a4dd-8cea89d355b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1b66dc5-759e-5b16-9d35-fc67c34a4499" :question/math-academy-id "q-38466" :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/79/79326f0f463d3b26db4ab497045fa35aa5aa7fb68be6fd79b5ebd633a4d6646f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=y\\,⟹\\,y=x,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. Its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/44/4452d48cac6bd387e8f7f27a9aedfbe51f9c5e478a5288f889aa3378c4805d34.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}[y]_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-{e}^{-4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2ea8c9b7-08dd-5aab-ac0c-8a662422b3d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32fbf319-3234-58a8-95fb-c53b859ed56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348902} {:answer/id #uuid "49820777-b895-52a8-9251-7d27144fa1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-{e}^{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349114} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6fed8a-5620-59e2-bea5-d6cea14b66c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349455} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe1cda9-fe65-5b36-99c7-49ccecf2e000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{16}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349473} {:answer/id #uuid "f9126db6-0a47-59b1-b887-454248d2f098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350634}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349473} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55be9a3-3b8c-5e73-9f00-cfe58e1d5da1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0524dddc-0711-51cc-bea5-211d12fb3f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-82296" :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{8}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d5/d5d777577f85cd577ce35bfd3a99c1e2218622d1856f4fe7ee4afe1cfd2c9f86.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 8,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. Its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05ec37a8f4fd33c05a2438df5c5fbc697f8b560fde0b9fe73922a0db56a09c2c.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}[y]_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{{x}^{4}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{16}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2ef3a076-a0cb-5e87-9202-566dbdfa7430" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad97339-5570-5d24-b1fb-77ec680fac1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2-y}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544142} {:answer/id #uuid "55f54e57-e18b-5d0c-9c19-13b3bfe26010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544276} {:answer/id #uuid "6a97f4e4-20e0-58ad-87bc-d933151f4bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544319} {:answer/id #uuid "bcca3de2-d84e-525d-b4d1-8d4e8c0a5627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y}}{\\int }_{2-y}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544487} {:answer/id #uuid "c31f4f8b-4dd6-5e15-bb60-2549b490b990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544319} :answer-field/id #uuid "d330fe8c-c0b4-581d-86c1-4285baf2910e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "437b2f48-85ba-532a-9ac5-52a828cb51ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-82254" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/47/472aa6b0afe20f7c1902446102265f758637c9ec00eb985bf38a658dc92f3fe5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=2-x\\,⟹\\,x=2-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 2-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/77/77b59ce5c40514338b0d23a31e7028c7fa0f7343aad018576301fcc4ec441ac1.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b59367e0-e2c1-5b73-8886-7978e62c8aa2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d78785e-c462-55f8-9982-52dc361d2a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544150} {:answer/id #uuid "3160ccd4-b4fb-549a-a695-6ba7523ae759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544196} {:answer/id #uuid "bce3792a-58d1-5db4-971f-0dd4799c18a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-y}}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544489} {:answer/id #uuid "d96c9ebe-385a-5aa9-8cce-0af1acc3fe17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544554} {:answer/id #uuid "e027944a-9ba9-531c-961b-bd1358b11e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544554} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad3fdbb4-05c4-5223-8593-ecad3e3ec912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c3ba9c34-ab2d-5d40-bb57-ca2ed5ef7608" :question/math-academy-id "q-82347" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ab/abf9e5dda9e98caac491aa5d94b3e2ae3f08f90d6bd490762adabc1437ac49d9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}-1\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type II regions. \n\n![](images/a4/a45e4b63b1eff23617abcbe33ce522652b20de6c817dfde2d9cdda52d12bb778.png)\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$  is \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\sqrt[3]{y+1}.$\n\nAlso, the right boundary of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\sqrt{1-y}.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y+1}\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}-1}^{1-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{y+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b6285502-e482-59e6-94a7-bdde0989b515" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544906}}] :activity/title "Changing the Order of Integration in Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544075}]}
